# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now — edit it, and shipshape will not
# overwrite it.
#
# Holds `a-permission-is-the-class-name`.
#
# **The permission IS the class name — not derived from it.** `SettleInvoice` needs
# `:SettleInvoice`. There is no transform, because every transform is lossy and a lossy
# transform collides: `FooBar` and `Foo::Bar` both underscore to `:foo_bar`, so a grant
# issued for one silently runs the other. Using the constant name itself is injective by
# construction — two classes cannot share a name, so two operations cannot share a
# permission, and no guard has to check it.
#
# **Renaming an operation renames the act, and that is a data migration.** Every stored
# grant of `:SettleInvoice` becomes `:SettleBooking`, written and reviewed like any other
# migration. That is not a cost this introduced — it is the cost a separate constant
# hides, while the code and the granted permission drift into naming two different things.
#
# **The catalogue is derived, never maintained.** Every operation answers `permission` and
# every workflow answers `permissions`, so the set the system recognises is read off the
# classes. No seed file to fall behind the code.
#
# **A human-readable name is data**, and so is the coarse bucket an administrator actually
# grants. Code carries one name; data carries the label, the description, and the grouping.
#
# **An operation with no actor says so by implementing `anonymous_call`.** Login, bootstrap
# and an unauthenticated upload run before anyone is identified, so there is nothing to
# check against. Naming the method is the declaration, which makes it a property of the
# operation rather than of whoever calls it — `grep -rn "def anonymous_call"` is the whole
# set, and it should only ever shrink.
#
# An actor who IS known but needs no grant is not this. Give it an ordinary permission
# granted to everyone, so it stays in the catalogue and can still be revoked for a
# suspended account.
module Permission
  # Every permission this application recognises, read off the classes.
  #
  # **Pass the doors you installed**, because a module cannot know which of them your
  # application uses:
  #
  #   Permission.catalogue(Command, Query, IoCommand, IoQuery, Workflow)
  #
  # Eager-load first outside production, or `descendants` answers only what has been
  # autoloaded so far and the catalogue comes back short — which is the one failure mode
  # that would make a coverage check pass while covering nothing:
  #
  #   Rails.application.eager_load!
  #
  # **What this is for.** An operation contained by no capability is unreachable —
  # fail-closed, correct, and invisible, surfacing as a refusal nobody can explain. Both
  # sides are enumerable, so the check is a comparison:
  #
  #   missing = Permission.catalogue(Command, Query, Workflow) - CapabilityRecord.permissions
  #   raise "not in any capability: #{missing.join(', ')}" if missing.any?
  #
  # Anonymous operations are left out: they are never granted, so demanding a capability
  # contain one would fail every check for ever. So are nameless classes — a `Class.new`
  # subclass has no constant, so it has no permission, and asking for one raises.
  #
  # **It raises on a workflow that declares no STEPS, deliberately.** That workflow would
  # otherwise refuse nothing at its first real call, in production; walking the catalogue at
  # boot is the cheapest place to find it.
  def self.catalogue(*doors)
    doors.flat_map { |door| door.descendants }
         .select(&:name)
         .reject(&:anonymous?)
         .flat_map { |operation| operation.respond_to?(:permissions) ? operation.permissions : [operation.permission] }
         .uniq
         .sort
  end

  def permission
    name.to_sym
  end

  def anonymous?
    method_defined?(:anonymous_call) || private_method_defined?(:anonymous_call)
  end

  # Absence of an actor is a caller's defect, never a quiet pass. A nil actor reaching
  # `may?` would raise somewhere less obvious, and a nil actor taken to mean "public" would
  # be the fail-open this module exists to prevent.
  def permits?(actor)
    return true if anonymous?
    raise ArgumentError, "#{name} requires an actor" if actor.nil?

    actor.may?(permission)
  end

  # Raised where an operation answers no envelope — a query says what it found or raises,
  # so a refusal cannot come back as a value without every caller unwrapping one.
  class Refused < StandardError
    def initialize(permission)
      super("not permitted: #{permission}")
    end
  end
end
